<TopicPage slug="subjects/business/intermediate/operations-and-supply-chains">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Marketing promises; <strong>operations delivers</strong> — turning plans into products
    and services by managing flow through constrained systems under uncertainty. It is the
    least glamorous function and the one whose arithmetic decides whether the business
    model can execute at all: how work is organised, where costs bite, what "quality"
    costs, and where in the world the work should physically happen. The unit-economics
    lesson priced one unit; this page widens the lens to the whole production system —
    and to the supply chains that connect your firm to everyone else's.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="constraints, buffers, bullwhip, and built-in quality" widget="equationStep" config={opsCfg} />

  <h2>Production of goods and services</h2>
  <p>
    Three organising methods cover most production. <strong>Job production</strong> makes
    one-off, customised items — a wedding dress, a bridge, bespoke software — with high
    skill, high unit cost, and total flexibility. <strong>Batch production</strong> makes
    groups of identical items before switching — a bakery's morning bread, afternoon
    cakes; a factory's red cars before blue — trading flexibility for efficiency, with
    switching ("set-up") time as the tax. <strong>Flow (mass) production</strong> moves
    items continuously down a line — bottling plants, car assembly — achieving the
    lowest unit cost at the price of rigidity and enormous capital. The choice is not
    aesthetic: it sets unit costs, the skills you must hire, and how fast you can change
    what you sell. Services complicate the picture in a characteristic way: they are
    <em>intangible</em> (can't be inspected before buying), <em>inseparable</em>
    (produced and consumed together — the flight and the flying happen simultaneously),
    <em>perishable</em> (an empty airline seat tonight is inventory destroyed at boarding)
    and <em>variable</em> (yesterday's service was good; today's depends on who was
    rostered). Those four properties are why airlines invented dynamic pricing, why
    hotels discount unsold rooms, and why service quality lives in training and process
    design rather than inspection.
  </p>

  <h2>Inventory: the buffer with a bill</h2>
  <p>
    Inventory exists because supply and demand refuse to synchronise — but it is the most
    misunderstood asset in business, because it is simultaneously a <em>service</em>
    (availability when customers arrive) and a <em>cost</em> (cash tied up, storage,
    insurance, spoilage, obsolescence — the total is conventionally estimated at
    20–30 per cent of the item's value per year). Two classical results price the
    trade-off. The <strong>economic order quantity</strong> (EOQ) balances ordering cost
    against holding cost: order too often and ordering costs eat you; too rarely and
    carrying costs do — the optimum ordering quantity grows with the square root of
    demand, not with demand itself, which is the non-obvious part. The
    <strong>newsvendor problem</strong> prices uncertainty itself: a newspaper seller
    facing one selling season must choose stock before demand is known — overstock and
    you write off leftovers, understock and you lose the margin on missed sales. The
    optimal rule balances the two costs (order more as the margin of a sale rises and
    the cost of leftover falls — the mathematics behind how fashion retailers and
    bakeries size their morning stock). Add a <strong>reorder point</strong> — the
    inventory level that triggers a new order, set to cover expected demand during the
    supplier's lead time plus a <em>safety stock</em> for its variability — and you have
    the machinery every warehouse runs on. Lean's radical claim is that most of this
    buffer is organisational scar tissue: uncertainty you could remove by fixing the
    process instead of insuring against it.
  </p>
  <p>
    The counter-movement has its own name and its own decade: <strong>just-in-case</strong>.
    Lean inventory cut costs for forty years — then COVID-19, the 2021 Suez Canal
    blockage (a grounded ship halted roughly a tenth of world trade for six days) and
    the semiconductor shortages showed the fragility: car manufacturers, running JIT to
    the month, idled entire factories over components worth a few dollars. The
    professional synthesis is that <strong>resilience is insurance with a premium</strong>:
    buffer stock, dual-sourced components, regional suppliers and visible pipelines all
    cost real money in good times and pay off catastrophically in bad ones. Which premium
    is worth paying is now a board-level question, not an operations footnote — and
    closely related is the <strong>make-or-buy</strong> question: outsource a function or
    run it yourself? Unit price is the lazy answer; the honest one weighs control,
    capability-building, coordination cost and dependency. Hidden coordination costs are
    why "simple" outsourcing projects overrun, and loss of capability is why firms that
    outsourced their engineering sometimes found they had outsourced their future.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> lean production means producing less, or cutting staff.
    Lean (from the Toyota Production System, named by researchers at MIT) means producing
    <em>exactly what is needed, when needed, with zero waste</em> — where waste includes
    overproduction, waiting, transport, excess inventory, motion, defects and unused
    talent. Its most famous tool, <strong>just-in-time (JIT)</strong>, arranges for
    components to arrive as they are used: inventory approaches zero, capital is freed,
    and defects surface immediately instead of hiding in a warehouse — the entire
    philosophy rests on quality being high enough that zero buffer is survivable.
  </p>

  <h2>Costs, scale and break-even</h2>
  <p>
    Operations costs split into <strong>fixed</strong> (rent, salaries, machinery lease —
    paid whatever output is) and <strong>variable</strong> (materials, packaging, hourly
    labour — rising with each unit). Their sum is total cost, and the split decides the
    risk profile of the business. <strong>Break-even analysis</strong> finds the output at
    which profit crosses zero: each unit sold contributes its <em>contribution</em> —
    price minus variable cost — toward covering fixed costs, so
    break-even output = fixed costs ÷ contribution. Work it: fixed costs 10,000 a month,
    price 25, variable cost 15 — contribution 10, break-even 1,000 units. Sell 900 and
    the month loses 1,000; sell 1,300 and profit is 3,000; every unit past 1,000 drops
    its whole 10 to the bottom line. The same arithmetic exposes <strong>operating
    leverage</strong>: shift those fixed costs up (buy the automated machine) and
    break-even rises — 15,000 ÷ contribution — while profit per unit above it rises too.
    High-fixed-cost businesses are amplified versions of themselves: brilliant at scale,
    fragile below it. One companion number completes the toolkit: the
    <strong>margin of safety</strong> — how far sales can fall before break-even is
    breached (selling 1,400 against a break-even of 1,000 gives a 29 per cent cushion).
    Businesses, like bridges, are rated by how much they can lose before failing.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Economies of scale</strong> are the reason size itself is a strategy:
    <em>purchasing</em> (bulk discounts), <em>technical</em> (bigger machines run
    proportionally cheaper), <em>managerial</em> (specialists replace generalists),
    <em>marketing</em> (a national campaign spread over more units) and <em>financial</em>
    (big firms borrow cheaper). The <strong>minimum efficient scale</strong> is the
    output past which unit costs stop falling — it tells you how many firms an industry
    can support (car makers: few, hence oligopoly; hairdressers: many, hence
    fragmentation). Beyond a point scale turns against you — <strong>diseconomies</strong>:
    coordination overhead, slow decisions, lost employees-in-a-company feeling. Growth
    past that point buys complexity, not efficiency.
  </p>

  <h2>Achieving quality</h2>
  <p>
    Quality has two competing philosophies. <strong>Quality control</strong> inspects
    output at the end and catches defects before customers do — after they have been
    paid to produce. <strong>Quality assurance</strong> builds quality into the process so
    defects don't arise — the cheaper philosophy, because the cost of catching a defect
    rises roughly tenfold at each stage it travels (the "1–10–100 rule": a part fixed at
    design costs 1, at production 10, in the customer's hands 100 — recall campaigns,
    reputational damage, litigation). W. Edwards Deming's insight — that most defects are
    <em>system</em> faults, not worker faults — became the engine of post-war Japanese
    manufacturing: <strong>total quality management (TQM)</strong> makes every employee
    responsible for improving the process, and <strong>kaizen</strong> (continuous
    improvement) treats tiny daily improvements as compounding assets, the way interest
    compounds money. Certification schemes (ISO 9000) formalise the discipline for
    customers who cannot watch the process themselves. The punchline that surprises
    newcomers: <strong>quality is usually the cost-cutter</strong>, because rework,
    scrap, returns and lost customers are quietly among the largest lines in any
    operation's cost base.
  </p>

  <h2>Location decisions</h2>
  <p>
    Where work happens is a long-lived, expensive-to-undo decision, and the factors
    weight differently by business. Manufacturers weigh proximity to <em>inputs</em>
    (heavy, bulky or perishable materials pull plants toward them — steel near ore,
    fish-packing near ports), to <em>markets</em> (when the product is heavier, more
    fragile or more expensive to ship than the inputs — bottling water where people
    drink it), <em>labour</em> (cost, skills, unions), <em>land</em> (price, space to
    expand), <em>infrastructure</em> (roads, ports, power, broadband) and
    <em>government</em> (tax incentives, grants, regulation, planning permission).
    Services and knowledge work weigh customer access and talent pools above all — which
    is why tech firms cluster where talent clusters, self-reinforcingly. The classic
    quantitative method scores each candidate site on weighted factors; the professional
    warning is that the weights are where the politics hide. The modern story is the
    reshuffling of <strong>globalisation</strong>: decades of offshoring to lowest-cost
    locations, then a partial reversal — <em>nearshoring</em> (closer but costlier),
    <em>friend-shoring</em> (aligned countries), and reshoring driven by fragility,
    tariffs and logistics shocks. Location has quietly become a risk-management question
    as much as a cost question.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> the cheapest location is the best location. Unit-cost
    comparisons routinely omit coordination costs (managing an operation 8,000 km away),
    quality variance, lead times (which inflate the inventory you must carry), currency
    risk and the political risk of depending on one geography. The firms that repatriated
    production after 2020 were not discovering sentiment; they were discovering the
    missing line items.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    Four expert threads. <strong>The Theory of Constraints</strong> (Goldratt, 1984):
    every system has one bottleneck, and the system's throughput equals the bottleneck's
    throughput — so the only leverage is there. His operating rhythm — <em>identify</em>
    the constraint, <em>exploit</em> it (never idle, never processing defects),
    <em>subordinate</em> everything else to it, <em>elevate</em> it (invest), then find
    the next constraint — is one of those rare business ideas that generalises
    cleanly to software teams, hospitals and kitchens. <strong>Queueing theory</strong>
    quantifies why 100 per cent utilisation is a target worth fearing: waiting times
    explode non-linearly as utilisation approaches capacity (a system at 90 per cent
    busy has roughly double the queue of one at 80 — and near-infinite queues at 99).
    The same mathematics runs emergency rooms, call centres and CPU schedulers: build in
    slack, or the system spends its slack in the worst possible way, as queues.
    <strong>The bullwhip effect</strong>: small wobbles in consumer demand amplify
    violently as you move up the chain — each layer orders a bit more against its own
    uncertainty, adds safety stock, and passes a bigger signal upstream (the beer game
    simulation makes students feel it in an afternoon); the cure is transparency — point-of-sale
    data flowing upstream, vendor-managed inventory — because shared information is
    cheaper than buffered stock. And <strong>Goodhart's law</strong> ("when a measure
    becomes a target, it ceases to be a good measure"): operational metrics are gamed
    the moment they matter — call centres optimised for average handling time transfer
    callers back to the queue; hospitals optimised for waiting times reclassify
    patients. The design rule: measure outcomes the customer would recognise, and pair
    every efficiency metric with a quality metric so the system cannot win one by
    losing the other.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Goldratt, The Goal; Womack &amp; Jones, Lean Thinking; Simchi-Levi, Designing and Managing the Supply Chain; Deming, Out of the Crisis.</p>
</TopicPage>
